-- X20 (SHADOW-SEMANTICS S1-PRELUDE (b)) -- x19's member-RENAME twin, opposite value.
--
-- The two recovery spellings are not interchangeable, and this cell is the one that says so.
--
--   PINNED: `(False, False)` -- BOTH components are the module's own `eq`.
--   x19's `(False, True)` is the same program with `import core as C` / `C.eq` in place of
--     the rename.  Transcribing either value onto the other cell destroys the distinction
--     this pair exists to hold.
--
-- WHY the rename does not escape: a method is global-by-name -- one dispatch cell per name,
-- never a per-module binding -- so an alias of a method is a second SPELLING, not a second
-- binding.  `renameAliasedMethods` rewrites `eqM` back to `eq` before any resolution runs,
-- and `eq` is then whatever this module's scope says it is, which here is the standalone.
-- This is GENERAL, not a property of the prelude: the same rewrite governs a rename of any
-- module's method.  `docs/spec/SYNTAX.md`'s alias table states it as a consequence.
--
-- A reader tempted to call this a defect should note what the alternative costs: not
-- de-aliasing a renamed method leaves the renamed reference bound to nothing, which is why
-- the rewrite has two halves that must agree (`compiler/types/typecheck.mdk`,
-- `renameAliasedMethods`).
--
-- VALUES HAND-DERIVED FROM THE RULE ABOVE, NOT CAPTURED: at the time this was written the
-- rename did not bind at all (`Unbound variable: eqM. Did you mean 'eq'`).
import core.{eq as eqM}

eq : Int -> Int -> Bool
eq _ _ = False

main = println (eq 1 1, eqM 1 1)
